  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nStorm Potential Sunday:\n\nAn upstream phased low level trough over the Northern Plains \nwill spread accompanying anomalous low level moisture (SPC RAOB \nClimatology: BIS, ABR) east through the overnight hours into Sunday \nmorning along a low level jet evident in Central Plains VWPs of 40 \nto 50kts. While increasing moisture and steepened mid level lapse \nrates increase local airmass instability, accompanying anomalous low \nlevel temperatures associated with an elevated mixed layer from \n850mb to 700mb poking northeast towards the Upper Mississippi River \nValley limits confidence in widespread storm chances through Sunday. \nFurthermore, given the lower late-summmer sun angle, duration and \nstrength of surface diurnal heating decreases confidence in reaching \nsurface convective temperatures. Therefore, given a lack of a \nwidespread, strong atmospheric forcing mechanism and weak passing \nupper level perturbation pulses, a limited window of storm potential \nis seen in high resolution model soundings through late Sunday \nafternoon into Sunday night. While elevated nature of storms would \nlimit severe hazards to large hail and damaging winds, overall \nconfidence in storms becoming severe remains low (<5%).\n\nStorm Potential Monday:\n\nPhasing and subsequent progression of the synoptic pattern \nprogresses the low level trough axis of drier air through Monday \nmorning. While the frontal boundary could potentially provide \nadditional forcing, ageostrophic subsidence in the equatorial exit \nregion of quasi-zonal amplifying upper level jet streak limits \nwidespread storm/precipitation chances through Monday locally. \nTherefore, while trend in Medium-Range Real-time AI convective \nhazard forecasts locally peaks Monday (5-25%), expect a downward \ntrend based on more recent, high resolution model forecasts. \n\nPeriodic Storm Chances, Above Normal Temperatures This Week:\n\nOther than periodic storm chances through the week, main forecast \nimpact will be above normal temperatures as long term global \nensembles and their AI/deterministic counterparts suggest persistent \nsynoptic ridging causing repeat return flow into the Upper Midwest. \nCurrent NBM confidence suggests upper 80 to low 90 maximum \ntemperatures as the 70 degree isodrosotherm brushes the forecast \narea causing 50-70% probabilities for 95+ degree apparent \ntemperatures across the southern half of the forecast area from \nTuesday to Thursday. Although, aforementioned storm potential \nincreasing cloud cover causing lower temperatures to be realized \nshould also be taken into account as forecast hour nears.",
